from common import cite

REFERENCES = [
 dict(path='appendices/a-math.html',title='Math and notation you will use',kicker='Appendix A',n='A',kw='formula notation symbols Wilson standard error quantile power',
 lede='A compact reference for the formulas in the lessons. Each formula names its target and assumptions.',
 body='''<h2 id="symbols">Read the symbols in words</h2>
<table><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td>n</td><td>Number of observations or trials; identify the unit</td></tr><tr><td>k</td><td>Attempts in a reliability event, or repeats when explicitly defined</td></tr><tr><td>p, p̂</td><td>A success probability and its estimate from observed data</td></tr><tr><td>x̄, s</td><td>Sample mean and sample standard deviation</td></tr><tr><td>Σ</td><td>Add the indexed quantities</td></tr><tr><td>E[X], Var(X)</td><td>Expectation and variance of a random variable</td></tr><tr><td>P(A | B)</td><td>Probability of A given B</td></tr><tr><td>α, δ</td><td>A test’s error threshold and a specified effect size</td></tr><tr><td>z<sub>q</sub></td><td>q quantile of the standard normal distribution</td></tr></table>
<p>Use probabilities on a zero-to-one scale in formulas: three percentage points is 0.03. Multiply a difference by 100 to report it in percentage points. A hat marks an estimate; a bar marks an average.</p>
<h2 id="mean">Mean, variance, and standard error</h2>
<div class="math">x̄ = Σxᵢ / n<br>s² = Σ(xᵢ−x̄)² / (n−1)<br>SE(x̄) ≈ s/√n</div>
<p>Add and divide to form the mean. Square deviations from that mean and divide by n−1 to form sample variance. Its square root measures individual-observation spread; dividing that by √n estimates uncertainty in the mean for independent, identically distributed observations with finite variance. See <a href="../pages/05-spread-and-standard-error.html">lesson 05</a>.</p>
<h2 id="wilson">Wilson interval</h2>
<div class="math">p̂ = successes/n; z = 1.9599639845 for a two-sided 95% interval<br>D = 1 + z²/n<br>Center = (p̂ + z²/(2n))/D<br>Half-width = z√[p̂(1−p̂)/n + z²/(4n²)]/D<br>Endpoints = Center ± Half-width</div>
<p>The center is adjusted away from the raw observed proportion, and the interval is not generally symmetric around p̂. This is a binomial score interval, assuming an appropriate iid binary sample. It does not model clustering. '''+cite('wilson')+'''
See <a href="../pages/06-confidence-intervals.html">lesson 06</a>.</p>
<h2 id="difference">Paired differences</h2>
<div class="math">dᵢ = Bᵢ − Aᵢ; estimated change = Σdᵢ/n<br>Estimated SE(change) = s<sub>d</sub>/√n</div>
<p>Compute each pair’s difference first and analyze those differences. A normal approximation may be suitable for many independent pairs; a t procedure makes its own distributional assumptions. A task or cluster bootstrap is another approximate approach when its unit matches the design. See <a href="../pages/08-paired-comparisons.html">lesson 08</a>.</p>
<h2 id="planning">Planning an independent-pair comparison</h2>
<div class="math">n ≈ ceiling([(z<sub>1−α/2</sub> + z<sub>power</sub>)s<sub>d</sub>/δ]²)</div>
<p>Divide the assumed difference SD by the desired detectable effect, scale by the test and power thresholds, square, and round up. At alpha 0.05 and power 0.8, the two z values are about 1.960 and 0.842. This plans detection against zero under a large-sample model. See <a href="../pages/10-sample-size.html">lesson 10</a>.</p>
<h2 id="quantiles">The lab’s quantile convention</h2>
<p>Sort n values and index from zero. For quantile q, calculate h = (n−1)q. Linearly interpolate between the values at floor(h) and ceiling(h). If h is an integer, use that value. This type-7 convention applies to the lab’s percentile bootstrap and posterior-draw intervals; another convention can shift endpoints in small samples.</p>
<h2 id="reliability">Reliability and zero failures</h2>
<div class="math">At least one success in k iid attempts: 1−(1−p)ᵏ<br>Every attempt succeeds: pᵏ<br>Zero failures in n iid trials: one-sided 95% upper q = 1−0.05<sup>1/n</sup></div>
<p>Complement all failures to obtain at-least-one success; multiply success probabilities to obtain all-success probability. Solve the no-failure probability for a failure-rate upper bound. These are different questions, and all require the stated independence model. See <a href="../pages/19-reliability-and-rare-events.html">lesson 19</a>.</p>
<h2 id="beyond">Choose the method by the question</h2>
<table><tr><th>Question</th><th>Starting point</th><th>Check first</th></tr><tr><td>One binary rate</td><td>Wilson or exact binomial interval</td><td>Sampling and independence</td></tr><tr><td>Same tasks, two versions</td><td>Paired differences</td><td>Matching and missing outcomes</td></tr><tr><td>Tasks share repositories</td><td>Cluster-aware analysis</td><td>Independent groups and target weighting</td></tr><tr><td>Unequal sampled categories</td><td>Design-aware weighting</td><td>Target mix and stratum design</td></tr><tr><td>Equivalence</td><td>Predeclared margins and appropriate tests</td><td>Not merely nonsignificance</td></tr><tr><td>Continuous monitoring</td><td>Planned sequential method</td><td>Permitted stopping and dependency assumptions</td></tr></table>'''),
 dict(path='appendices/b-explorers.html',title='Interactive calculators',kicker='Appendix B',n='B',kw='interactive calculator Wilson power reliability confidence interval',
 lede='Change one assumption at a time. These local calculators show the arithmetic and name the model it depends on.',
 body='''<noscript><p>The calculators need JavaScript. The rest of the course works without it; use the equivalent Python commands in Appendix C.</p></noscript>
<h2 id="rate">What does sample size do to an interval?</h2>
<p>Start at 72/100. Then try 720/1,000, which keeps the observed rate fixed while increasing independent evidence. Finally try 10/10 and notice the lower bound. Read <a href="../pages/06-confidence-intervals.html">lesson 06</a> before interpreting the result.</p>
<form class="explorer" data-calculator="wilson"><label for="passes">Observed passes<input id="passes" name="passes" type="number" min="0" max="1000000000" step="1" value="72" required></label><label for="trials">Independent binary trials<input id="trials" name="trials" type="number" min="1" max="1000000000" step="1" value="100" required></label><button type="submit">Calculate interval</button><output aria-live="polite"></output><svg class="interval-plot" viewBox="0 0 560 100" role="img" aria-label="Wilson interval on a zero to one hundred percent scale"></svg><p>Two-sided 95% Wilson interval. Assumes independent Bernoulli observations from the declared population; excludes measurement bias and distribution shift.</p></form>
<h2 id="power">How many paired tasks?</h2>
<p>Change a three-point target to six points. Then change the assumed SD of paired differences. The target is detection against zero, not a guarantee that the gain exceeds a deployment margin.</p>
<form class="explorer" data-calculator="power"><label for="delta">Difference to detect, in percentage points<input id="delta" name="delta" type="number" min="0.001" max="100" step="any" value="3" required></label><label for="sd">SD of paired differences, on the 0–1 score scale<input id="sd" name="sd" type="number" min="0.0001" max="2" step="any" value="0.5" required></label><button type="submit">Plan sample size</button><output aria-live="polite"></output><p>Large-sample approximation: independent pairs, two-sided alpha 0.05, power 0.8. Input SD must come from a justified model or pilot. Clustering and repeated looks require another design.</p></form>
<h2 id="event">What does “three attempts” mean?</h2>
<p>Compare at-least-one success with all-attempt success at a fixed hypothetical p. This is probability arithmetic for a known p, not an interval or the finite-sample pass@k estimator.</p>
<form class="explorer" data-calculator="reliability"><label for="p">Hypothetical per-attempt success probability<input id="p" name="p" type="number" min="0" max="1" step="any" value="0.8" required></label><label for="k">Attempts on one task<input id="k" name="k" type="number" min="1" max="10000" step="1" value="3" required></label><button type="submit">Compare success events</button><output aria-live="polite"></output><p>Assumes independent attempts with the same p for this task. Adaptive retries and shared state need an explicit model. Task heterogeneity means you must compute within tasks before averaging.</p></form>
<p>All calculations run in this page. There are no requests, accounts, or stored results.</p>'''),
 dict(path='appendices/c-command-reference.html',title='Command reference and data schema',kicker='Appendix C',n='C',kw='terminal command CLI Python lab schema JSONL offline',
 lede='Every learner command uses local Python and the standard library. Run from the statistics-tut directory.',
 body='''<h2 id="start">Start here</h2><pre class="term"><code>python3 --version
python3 labs/stats_lab.py --help
python3 labs/stats_lab.py quiz</code></pre>
<p>Use Python 3.9 or newer. The quiz accepts 1, 2, 3, or q and prints explanations. Ctrl-C stops an experiment. No command in this table requires an API key, package installation, or network access.</p>
<h2 id="commands">A small working set</h2>
<table><tr><th>Command after <code>python3 labs/stats_lab.py</code></th><th>Purpose</th><th>Read</th></tr><tr><td><code>rate 72 100</code></td><td>95% Wilson interval</td><td>06</td></tr><tr><td><code>sample --trials 100 --p 0.7</code></td><td>Sampling variation and coverage</td><td>03–06</td></tr><tr><td><code>compare</code></td><td>Paired task bootstrap and exact discordance test</td><td>07–09</td></tr><tr><td><code>compare --data labs/data/clustered_tasks.jsonl --unit cluster</code></td><td>Keep repository groups intact</td><td>11</td></tr><tr><td><code>compare --data labs/data/clustered_tasks.jsonl --unit cluster --slice sensitive</code></td><td>Inspect the sensitive category</td><td>12</td></tr><tr><td><code>power --sd 0.5 --delta 0.03</code></td><td>Approximate independent-pair count</td><td>10</td></tr><tr><td><code>bayes 8 10 --alpha 1 --beta 1</code></td><td>Posterior and prior sensitivity</td><td>16</td></tr><tr><td><code>multiple --tests 20</code></td><td>Multiple null tests</td><td>17</td></tr><tr><td><code>peek</code></td><td>Broken repeated-testing rule</td><td>18</td></tr><tr><td><code>reliability 8 10 3</code></td><td>At-least-one and all-k estimators</td><td>19</td></tr><tr><td><code>rare 100</code></td><td>Upper rate bound after zero failures</td><td>19</td></tr><tr><td><code>correlate --x "1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8" --y "3 1 2 5 4 6 7 20"</code></td><td>Pearson, Spearman, and an exact permutation p-value</td><td>24</td></tr><tr><td><code>correlate --candidate unit-003</code></td><td>One fixture candidate with a randomized permutation test</td><td>24–25</td></tr><tr><td><code>screen</code></td><td>3,000 simulated null candidates: raw, Bonferroni, and BH counts, plus a held-out split</td><td>24–25</td></tr><tr><td><code>screen --real 30 --effect 1</code></td><td>Same screen with planted associations</td><td>24</td></tr><tr><td><code>screen --data labs/data/candidates.jsonl</code></td><td>Screen the sixty-candidate fixture by permutation</td><td>25</td></tr></table>
<p>Use a subcommand’s <code>--help</code> for options. Randomized commands accept <code>--seed</code>; compare also accepts <code>--resamples</code>, and correlate and screen accept <code>--permutations</code>. The correlate command enumerates every label ordering when n ≤ 8 and draws random orderings otherwise. The data path defaults are resolved relative to the script, so <code>compare</code> also works when invoked from another directory. An explicitly supplied relative path is relative to your current directory.</p>
<h2 id="schema">One task per line</h2>
<pre><code>{"task_id":"repo-01-task-0", "cluster_id":"repo-01",
 "slice":"routine", "a":[0,1,1], "b":[1,1,1]}</code></pre>
<p>The example is wrapped for reading; each JSONL record occupies one line in the file. IDs and slice labels must be nonempty strings, task IDs unique, and score arrays nonempty lists of integer zeroes and ones. A/B attempt counts must match within a task. The schema intentionally rejects unknown scores instead of silently interpreting them as failures.</p>
<p>The CLI computes the mean within each task and gives each task equal weight. It does not require attempts to correspond by seed. With cluster mode, related task differences stay together, and the total task-weighted statistic is recomputed in each resample. The <a href="../labs/data/README.md">fixture notes</a> describe all three datasets.</p>
<h2 id="candidate-schema">One candidate per line</h2>
<pre><code>{"candidate_id":"unit-003", "activation":[0.51, -1.2, ...],
 "label":[-0.45, -1.86, ...], "planted":true}</code></pre>
<p>The <code>activation</code> and <code>label</code> lists must have the same length and at least three finite numbers. The optional <code>planted</code> flag lets the fixture report which rejections were false; omit it for your own data, and the screen output omits those columns. The screen command tests every candidate against its own label column; the fixture repeats one shared label in each record so that each line stands alone.</p>
<h2 id="limits">When to go beyond the teaching lab</h2>
<p>General weights, ordinal scores, complex missingness, few-cluster corrections, crossed dependency structures, and production-scale data are outside this small implementation. The bootstrap is a nominal 95% percentile approximation. It is provided so you can read the code and understand what is resampled, not as a universal statistical package.</p>
<p>Command outputs used in authoring are retained in <a href="../research/verify/lab-transcript.txt">the verification transcript</a>. For the formulas, see <a href="../labs/stats.py">stats.py</a>; for CLI policy, see <a href="../labs/stats_lab.py">stats_lab.py</a>.</p>'''),
 dict(path='appendices/d-reading-map.html',title='Reading map and next steps',kicker='Appendix D',n='D',kw='sources study route books reading prerequisites further learning',
 lede='Read more when a real evaluation question needs it. The course stands alone; these sources add depth.',
 body='''<h2 id="paths">Choose a route through this course</h2>
<ul><li><strong>First statistics course:</strong> read 00–23 in order and predict each reasoning-check answer before revealing it; add 24–25 when you start looking inside models.</li><li><strong>Review a result this week:</strong> read 01, 04–09, 11–14, and 23; return for sample-size and selection lessons before planning the next run.</li><li><strong>Design an experiment:</strong> read 01, 04, 10–14, 17–21, then fill the report template before collecting outcomes.</li><li><strong>Screen candidate neurons or features:</strong> read 09, 11, 17, then 24–25, and write the held-out prediction and the control before computing a single correlation.</li></ul>
<h2 id="foundation">Foundations and calculation</h2>
<p>The NIST/SEMATECH e-Handbook gives formal accounts of <a href="https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/eda/section3/eda352.htm">mean intervals</a>, <a href="https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/prc/section2/prc241.htm">proportion intervals</a>, and <a href="https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/prc/section3/prc311.htm">paired observations</a>. Read after lessons 05–09. Reproduce one formula with the local lab before moving to another method.</p>
<p><a href="https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.stats.bootstrap.html">SciPy’s bootstrap documentation</a> is useful when moving from the teaching implementation to a maintained scientific library. Pay attention to paired indices, the chosen interval method, and warnings. A library default is not an analysis plan.</p>
<h2 id="evals">Statistics in evals</h2>
<p><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2411.00640v1">Miller’s Adding Error Bars to Evals</a> is the focused next reading for task variation, repeated answers, paired contrasts, and planning. Bring the distinctions from lessons 01 and 11: fixed task sets, new task populations, and independent groups need different interpretations.</p>
<p><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2107.03374v2">Chen et al., Evaluating Large Language Models Trained on Code</a>, section 2.1, develops the pass@k estimator. Compare its sampling event with your application’s output-selection and retry policy.</p>
<h2 id="judgment">Statistical judgment</h2>
<p>The <a href="https://www.amstat.org/asa/files/pdfs/p-valuestatement.pdf">ASA p-value principles</a> are a short companion to lesson 09. Practice rewriting a threshold-only result as an estimate, uncertainty statement, and practical decision.</p>
<p>For ongoing monitoring, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.08240">Howard and colleagues’ confidence-sequence paper</a> gives the formal target for time-uniform inference. It is advanced reading. Use lesson 18 to recognize the need; do not deploy a sequential method solely from its name.</p>
<h2 id="screening">Correlation, permutation, and screening</h2>
<p>The SciPy pages for <a href="https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.stats.pearsonr.html">pearsonr</a>, <a href="https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.stats.spearmanr.html">spearmanr</a>, and <a href="https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.stats.permutation_test.html">permutation_test</a> document the coefficients, the normality assumption behind the default p-values, and the pairings permutation type that the lab’s <code>correlate</code> command imitates. Read after lesson 24, then compare the lab’s exact enumeration with the library’s result on the same eight pairs.</p>
<p>For many candidates, <a href="https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.stats.false_discovery_control.html">false_discovery_control</a> and statsmodels’ <a href="https://www.statsmodels.org/stable/generated/statsmodels.stats.multitest.multipletests.html">multipletests</a> implement Benjamini–Hochberg and its dependence-robust variants; both cite <a href="https://doi.org/10.1111/j.2517-6161.1995.tb02031.x">Benjamini and Hochberg (1995)</a>. The scikit-learn <a href="https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/cross_validation.html">cross-validation guide</a> is the standard account of why a held-out set must stay unused. Lesson 25 applies both to explanation scoring; the <a href="https://github.com/openai/automated-interpretability/blob/main/neuron-explainer/neuron_explainer/explanations/scoring.py">released scoring code</a> shows the correlation score as implemented.</p>
<h2 id="measurement">Grader and forecast quality</h2>
<p>The scikit-learn guides to <a href="https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/model_evaluation.html">metrics</a> and <a href="https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/calibration.html">calibration</a> explain implementation conventions. Check positive-label orientation, weighting, undefined metrics, and independent validation.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://statproofbook.github.io/P/bin-post.html">Beta–Binomial posterior derivation</a> lets you verify lesson 16 algebraically. For repeated heterogeneous tasks, the next topic is hierarchical modeling, not treating every attempt as an iid draw by habit.</p>
<h2 id="siblings">Connect statistics back to the system</h2>
<ul><li><a href="../../../evals/evals-tut/index.html">Understanding LLM Evals</a>: measurement validity, grader design, contamination, and evaluation practice.</li><li><a href="../../../networking/networking-tut/index.html">Networking for LLM Evals</a>: deadlines, retries, concurrency, and missing results.</li><li><a href="../../../linux/linux-tut/index.html">Linux for Evals Engineers</a>: the execution environment, reproducibility, and harness integrity.</li><li><a href="../../../interpretability/interpretability-tut/index.html">Interpretability Methods and Rigor</a> (forthcoming): the prediction test applied to real neurons and features, with lessons 24–25 as its statistical prerequisite.</li></ul>
<p>These courses address different layers of the same evidence chain. Sound statistics depends on observations that were collected and graded under the policy you claim to have measured.</p>'''),
]
